WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.400
As any fan will tell you, Marvel's many&nbsp;
blockbusters are packed full of truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.200
incredible moments. But which ones are the most&nbsp;
electrifying of all? From devastating cliffhangers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.280
to epic fight scenes, these are the biggest&nbsp;
jaw-dropping moments in Marvel movie history.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.200
Everyone knew what they were in for with&nbsp;
Avengers: Infinity War. Earth's Mightiest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:20.880
Heroes would have a tough fight against Thanos,&nbsp;
and it was likely that they wouldn't get away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.320
without losing one or two members of the&nbsp;
team. But even if they didn't win, they'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.200
at least prolong the battle until the sequel.&nbsp;
Surely Thanos wouldn't actually win… right?

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.240
And yet, as Infinity War came to a shocking&nbsp;
close, Thanos' dream of ending half of all life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:39.040
in the universe was finally realized. Audiences&nbsp;
around the world watched in horror as everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:44.080
from Black Panther to Spider-Man dissolved into&nbsp;
dust before their very eyes. It was a snap heard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:48.880
around the universe — one that continues to echo&nbsp;
through the MCU to this day. This was something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:54.320
no comic book movie had even attempted before; it&nbsp;
was ambitious, terrifying, and utterly unexpected.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.960
Avengers: Endgame was a film that had to&nbsp;
follow Thanos' snap — by both meditating on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.480
the fallout of the tragedy and finding&nbsp;
a way to rise above it. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.560
it's a story packed to the rafters with great&nbsp;
moments, any one of which could stake a claim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:10.880
as the movie's best. From Captain Marvel's&nbsp;
late-game entrance to Tony's sacrifice to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.840
that iconic portal sequence… Well, they're all&nbsp;
great. But there's great, and then there's this.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.720
From the moment that hammer hovers, you know&nbsp;
exactly what's coming. Cap lifting Mjolnir&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:24.400
is a concept first toyed with all the way&nbsp;
back in Age of Ultron, and everyone knew it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.800
was coming eventually — but it's still such a&nbsp;
masterfully-executed payoff that you just can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.440
help but cheer. And it doesn't exactly hurt that&nbsp;
the ensuing showdown with Thanos is one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:38.240
most satisfying scenes in the whole MCU, either.&nbsp;
It really doesn't get much better than this.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:43.040
There were a whole heap of narrative challenges&nbsp;
to overcome in that first Avengers movie. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:46.800
film had to successfully convey Loki as&nbsp;
a villain big enough for all six heroes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.040
convincingly set up tension between them, and&nbsp;
then prove that they're a great team after all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:55.600
And then, amidst all that, The Avengers had to&nbsp;
pretty much redefine the character of the Hulk.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.720
Luckily, Marvel rose to the&nbsp;
occasion — and then some.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.320
Hulk's whole arc in the film, from&nbsp;
sad-sack scientist to reluctant hero,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.560
is magnificent, but his best moment comes when&nbsp;
Banner finally arrives at the final fight in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.360
New York. Surrounded by the city's ruins,&nbsp;
he utters arguably his most famous MCU line:&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:19.520
"Now might be a really good time for you to get&nbsp;
angry." "That's my secret, Cap. I'm always angry."

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:23.600
...And then he lets loose with a punch that&nbsp;
shows the Hulk for what he really is: raw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.320
power. Hulk's had plenty of great moments since&nbsp;
then, but none have been quite as potent as this.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:34.240
X2: X-Men United is among the best of Fox's X-Men&nbsp;
films, largely because it's a movie that retains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.720
what everyone liked about the first X-film, while&nbsp;
upping the ante in just about every respect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.960
It does this not only by keeping the focus on&nbsp;
Wolverine and Rogue, but also introducing new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.280
characters with dazzling abilities and raising&nbsp;
the stakes on almost every action set piece.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.320
While Magneto's prison escape is&nbsp;
a favorite scene for some fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.000
for others, the undisputed champion of&nbsp;
this film happens in its opening moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.080
when Nightcrawler breaks into the Oval Office&nbsp;
and attempts to assassinate the president.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.600
It's later revealed that Nightcrawler has&nbsp;
been brainwashed, and that his plight is at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:05.920
the center of a massive conspiracy to rid the&nbsp;
world of mutantkind. He also turns out to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.920
a perfectly nice guy who'd never deliberately&nbsp;
do what he did in the film's opening minutes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:13.840
But that doesn't stop the sequence from&nbsp;
packing a serious punch, and even today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.840
it remains one of the most thrilling action&nbsp;
scenes in the world of comic book movies.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:22.640
Since its release in 2004, Spider-Man 2 has&nbsp;
been followed by six other Spider-Man films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:26.640
four of which starred other actors as the&nbsp;
iconic web-slinger. But for many fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.520
Sam Raimi's sequel remains the gold standard for&nbsp;
both Spidey and superhero movies, largely thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:36.480
to its emotional core and superb action sequences.&nbsp;
This is best demonstrated in the still-legendary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:41.760
sequence in which Spider-Man has to stop a runaway&nbsp;
train after Doctor Octopus sabotages the controls.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.800
Though it would later be topped in&nbsp;
scope by Homecoming's ferry sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:49.200
Raimi directs the heck out of the train rescue,&nbsp;
in which Peter Parker, unmasked and desperate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.520
sticks his webbing to various passing buildings&nbsp;
in an effort to make a sling with his own body.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:56.880
But what really elevates this scene above&nbsp;
all the rest is the moment in which Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.120
passes out from sheer exhaustion and wakes&nbsp;
to find all the train passengers staring at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.120
him. They're so grateful for his help, and so&nbsp;
understanding of how important Spider-Man is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:09.600
to their city, that they hand back his mask&nbsp;
and promise to never tell anyone who he is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.800
It's a beautiful final touch&nbsp;
to a truly spectacular scene.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:18.480
Logan isn't a particularly complex film by comic&nbsp;
book movie standards. It doesn't introduce a horde&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:23.120
of new heroes, and it doesn't end with a massive,&nbsp;
CGI-heavy apocalyptic showdown. At its heart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:27.680
this is a movie about a man who's waiting to die&nbsp;
— and finds a reason to live a little longer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.040
Considering the heart of the movie is the&nbsp;
relationship between Logan and his young clone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:34.400
Laura, you'd be forgiven for expecting&nbsp;
some kind of action team-up between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:37.520
the two. But this doesn't occur&nbsp;
until the very end of the movie.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:40.640
When it finally does happen, however,&nbsp;
it's obvious that this is going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:44.400
more than just a fight scene. When&nbsp;
Laura realizes Logan has her back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.560
it's a tremendous emotional payoff. And&nbsp;
as Logan's body fills up with bullets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:52.640
it becomes clear that this isn't going to end&nbsp;
like other superhero fights, either. The whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.280
sequence is a tremendous combination of action&nbsp;
and emotional intensity, making for a truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:01.920
memorable climax… and setting up one of the&nbsp;
greatest endings in comic book movie history.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:06.480
Thor: Ragnarok is a film that sets out to&nbsp;
reimagine both Thor and the world around him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:10.480
As a result, it's a movie full of fresh sights&nbsp;
and sounds that offer a lighter — and yet more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:14.240
emotionally resonant — take on the god of&nbsp;
thunder. There are a number of absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:18.000
dazzling moments in the film, but they&nbsp;
don't get much better than that final fight.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:22.240
As Hela's armies close in on his friends, an&nbsp;
injured, one-eyed Thor is desperate for some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:26.640
kind of help. Then he thinks of his father's&nbsp;
words, realizes his power comes from within,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:30.080
and unleashes a lightning bolt&nbsp;
that finally slows Hela down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:34.320
Scored by Led Zeppelin's "Immigrant Song," Thor&nbsp;
makes the mother of all entrances as he joins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:38.720
the battle on the Bifrost. Throw in Valkyrie&nbsp;
and her firework strut, Loki's redemption,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:43.760
and Hulk suplexing Fenrir, and you've got the&nbsp;
recipe for one of the MCU's best fight scenes.

00:05:44.308 --> 00:05:47.680
Spider-Man: Far From Home establishes&nbsp;
that the MCU's Peter Parker does indeed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.440
have a spider-sense, which his aunt has&nbsp;
helpfully nicknamed "the Peter Tingle."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:54.880
He also apparently has the legendary&nbsp;
Parker luck, because just when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:58.640
think the kid has finally caught a break,&nbsp;
the film pulls the rug out from under him.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:02.000
By the end of Far From Home, everything&nbsp;
seems to be going well for Peter. He's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:05.360
in a relationship with MJ, all of his&nbsp;
friends made it home from Europe safe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:08.880
and he's cemented his place as&nbsp;
Tony Stark's successor in the MCU.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:13.440
That all comes crashing down, however, when&nbsp;
it's revealed via J. Jonah Jameson that Mysterio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:18.458
has framed Spider-Man for his apparent death…&nbsp;
and exposes his secret identity to the world.

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:20.640
"What the f-"

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:24.960
The long-awaited reintroduction of J. Jonah&nbsp;
Jameson to the MCU was enough to put jaws on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:29.120
the floor, but then the scene went further&nbsp;
by totally ruining Peter Parker's life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:30.720
This one's going to take some fixing.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:36.080
A film like Captain America: Civil War is more&nbsp;
or less purpose built for big surprise moments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:39.200
It introduced both Black Panther&nbsp;
and Spider-Man to the MCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:43.680
and pitted various heroes against each other&nbsp;
in a super v. super battle like no other.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:48.080
And that iconic airport scene's biggest moment&nbsp;
turned out to be the biggest in a very literal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:52.320
sense. This is the moment in which Scott Lang&nbsp;
reveals that he's capable of growing his body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:57.360
to a gigantic size. Seemingly out of nowhere,&nbsp;
Ant-Man grows several dozen feet and plucks War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:01.440
Machine right out of the air. And while he would&nbsp;
later reappear in both Ant-Man and the Wasp and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:06.080
Avengers: Endgame, this Giant-Man moment will&nbsp;
always hold a special place in fans' hearts.

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:09.760
Steve Rogers isn't a superhero&nbsp;
in the same sense that, say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:14.000
Thor or the Hulk is; nor is he an ordinary&nbsp;
man in the same way that Tony Stark is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:18.080
Steve Rogers' treatment with the Super Soldier&nbsp;
Serum has enhanced his body to the point that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:23.200
he's essentially reached maximum human potential&nbsp;
in terms of strength, speed, endurance, and more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:28.080
He's quite literally the best version of a normal&nbsp;
human being without any external enhancements.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.640
And because he has the heart and&nbsp;
determination of Steve Rogers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:34.400
he's able to push himself to the point where even&nbsp;
other enhanced humans wouldn't be able to go.

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:38.800
"I can do this all day." "Yeah, I know, I know."

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:43.680
This is perhaps best exemplified in Captain&nbsp;
America: Civil War, when Cap single-handedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:47.600
holds a helicopter in place, mid-takeoff,&nbsp;
for several seconds, using nothing but his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:52.320
sheer strength and his willpower. Even for Cap,&nbsp;
it's quite a feat, and Chris Evans' startling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:56.160
ability to make his biceps pop is enough&nbsp;
to make this sequence even more memorable.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:01.120
Captain Marvel arrived in the MCU at a time when&nbsp;
Thanos had already snapped, when the Avengers had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:06.160
assembled three times, and when characters such as&nbsp;
Vision and Giant-Man were practically commonplace.

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:09.680
Despite all of this, Captain Marvel had to&nbsp;
convince fans that they were witnessing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:14.000
creation of the MCU's most powerful hero —&nbsp;
someone stronger than virtually any other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.320
Avenger in the room. So how did the movie&nbsp;
do this? Simple. By having the hero defeat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:23.200
a whole army all by herself. After choosing to&nbsp;
defend the persecuted Skrulls, a fully-powered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:28.080
Carol Danvers takes out an entire Kree squadron&nbsp;
and then flies through a massive Kree warship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:32.640
destroying it in seconds and sending the surviving&nbsp;
bad guys packing. And from that point on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:36.480
there's absolutely no doubt that Carol&nbsp;
Danvers is a force to be reckoned with.

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:42.080
No one knew quite what to expect from Venom, the&nbsp;
first solo film to star the titular antihero.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:46.240
Would it be overly dark and gritty? So&nbsp;
self-serious that it became silly? Too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:49.760
focused on its creature to add any&nbsp;
real humanity? It wasn't easy to pin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:53.280
down from the marketing, either, which&nbsp;
is why the end result was so refreshing:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:56.880
a genuinely quirky blend of darkness,&nbsp;
action, and self-aware humor.

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:01.120
Venom begins as a sort of body horror film,&nbsp;
as Eddie Brock accidentally comes across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:04.880
symbiote, and then struggles to understand why&nbsp;
there's a voice in his head all of a sudden.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:08.800
It's an adjustment period that manifests itself&nbsp;
in all kinds of ways, but the most memorable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:13.120
one occurs when Eddie storms into a fancy San&nbsp;
Francisco restaurant in an effort to confront his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:17.680
former partner with his discoveries. Of course,&nbsp;
the Venom symbiote has other plans, and by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:21.840
end of the scene Eddie has submerged himself&nbsp;
in a live lobster tank. The whole scene just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:26.960
gets weirder when Eddie snatches up one of the&nbsp;
crustaceans and chows down on a raw seafood lunch.

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:31.120
Although Venom certainly has flashier moments&nbsp;
along the way, this is the one that set the tone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:36.480
for this particular superhero franchise: Funny,&nbsp;
unpredictable, and more than a little bizarre.

